<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  THIS FILE REPLACES UnityManifest.xml. It does not merge into it.

  That is the whole reason it needs an <activity> block. Unity uses this path
  as the unityLibrary module's manifest, so whatever is here is the ONLY thing
  that module contributes — and the previous version of this file declared a
  permission and an empty <application>, which silently deleted both of Unity's
  activity declarations. The build said so and nobody read it:

      warning: Unable to find Unity activity in manifest.
               Some attributes may not be set properly and need to be set
               manually.

  The artifact agreed: `aapt2 dump badging` printed no launchable-activity and
  the merged manifest contained zero <activity> elements. The APK installed and
  could not be started — no launcher icon, nothing to tap. Play would have
  accepted that upload.

  The activity below is Unity's own, copied from
  <unity>/PlaybackEngines/AndroidPlayer/Apk/UnityManifest.xml. It is the
  GameActivity variant because androidApplicationEntry is GameActivity, which
  is also why libgame.so is in the bundle and why android.app.lib_name must
  say "game". Switching Application Entry in Player Settings means switching
  this block to UnityPlayerActivity + @style/UnityThemeSelector.

  android:exported is stated rather than left to Unity's post-processing: it is
  mandatory for a component with an intent-filter from API 31, and this file is
  the reason that post-processing could not find the activity in the first
  place.

  VIBRATE is here because AndroidHaptics goes through the Android Vibrator
  service directly — Unity's Handheld.Vibrate() ignores its duration argument
  and fires the ~500ms system buzz, far too long for a game built on tapping.
  Unity injects the permission when it SEES Handheld.Vibrate() in the
  assemblies; it cannot see an AndroidJavaObject call, so without this line
  every haptic silently no-ops on device and nothing in the build reports it.

  INTERNET is deliberately absent (PlayerSettings.Android.forceInternetPermission
  = false), which is what makes the privacy policy's "this app makes no network
  requests" claim true rather than aspirational.

  Nothing here sets android:extractNativeLibs. An earlier version did, and it
  had no effect: Unity writes that attribute onto <application> itself and the
  shipped manifest read "true" regardless. An attribute that loses every merge
  is worse than absent, because it reads as a decision that was made.
-->
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.VIBRATE" />

    <application>
        <activity android:name="com.unity3d.player.UnityPlayerGameActivity"
                  android:theme="@style/BaseUnityGameActivityTheme"
                  android:exported="true">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>
            <meta-data android:name="unityplayer.UnityActivity" android:value="true" />
            <meta-data android:name="android.app.lib_name" android:value="game" />
        </activity>
    </application>
</manifest>
